    "result_text": "The full suite is just the one acceptance test class (the other two files are a base class and a helper) \u2014 and it passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the daily owner registration limit: creating an owner is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100+ owners already exist for that registration date. Following the existing per-city limit pattern, each piece went where it belongs:\n\n- **`DailyOwnerLimitException`** (new, `rest/validation/`) \u2014 carries `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` and `FIELD = \"registrationDate\"`, mirroring `CityOwnerLimitException`.\n- **`OwnerRepository.countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`** \u2014 new interface method, implemented in all three backends (`SpringDataOwnerRepository` via `@Query`, `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` via JPQL, `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` via SQL) so every persistence profile stays consistent.\n- **`ClinicService.countOwnersByRegistrationDate`** + impl \u2014 thin delegating service method alongside `countOwnersByCity`.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after the registration date is resolved (defaulting to today), it checks the count and throws `DailyOwnerLimitException`, reusing the existing normalize/validate flow.\n- **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 new handler translating the exception to a 429 with the offending field, matching the shape of the city-limit handler.\n\nThe check counts by the owner's own `registrationDate` (defaulting to `LocalDate.now()`), consistent with how the city check keys off the owner's own city.",
